/* QA: [cat, question, why he asks, model answer] */
const QA = [
["sci","Why a slit jet rather than a pinhole?","The single best filter question for whether you read his papers or read about them. If you cannot answer this, nothing else you say will land.","Three reasons, and they compound. First, density in a slit expansion falls off as 1/r rather than 1/r-squared, so collision density stays high much further downstream — one to two orders of magnitude more cooling collisions before the free-molecular freeze-in region, which is how you get rotational temperatures of 10 to 30 K. Second, the absorption path is centimetres along the slit instead of a millimetre across a pinhole, so absorbance scales up directly. Third, and the reason it's called sub-Doppler: if you propagate the probe beam along the slit, perpendicular to the expansion, the molecular velocity component along the laser nearly vanishes and Doppler broadening collapses to tens of MHz. Cold, long and narrow, all from one piece of geometry."],
["sci","Explain to me what makes cyclobutyl and cyclopentyl behave differently.","He published both. This tests whether you can hold two of his results side by side and extract the physics rather than reciting abstracts.","Same coordinate, opposite regimes. Both are out-of-plane ring puckering in a double-minimum potential, and what decides the outcome is the barrier height relative to the zero-point level. In cyclobutyl the barrier is shallow, so the wavefunction is strongly delocalized over both puckered minima and the molecule has vibrationally averaged C2v symmetry — it behaves like a planar pi radical even though the equilibrium geometry is puckered. In cyclopentyl the inversion barrier is around 3.7 kcal per mole, much larger than the effective moment of inertia for out-of-plane bending, so the wavefunction localizes near the C2 equilibrium geometry and the ground-state tunneling splitting drops below a MHz — unresolvable. The useful part is that tunneling splitting is a direct quantitative readout of barrier height, so the two molecules together calibrate the whole picture."],
["sci","What does the disappearance of C60 transitions actually tell you?","His flagship recent result, and a question that separates people who understand symmetry from people who have memorized a headline.","It is nuclear exchange statistics made visible. Carbon-12 nuclei are bosons with zero spin, so the total molecular wavefunction has to be symmetric under exchange of any two of the sixty identical nuclei. Combine that requirement with the icosahedral selection rules and certain rovibrational levels simply cannot exist — not weakly allowed, but zero statistical weight. So in the R-branch progression from J equals zero upward, specific lines are completely absent. That is why getting cold matters so much: C60's rotational constant is tiny, so at ordinary temperatures the populated levels carry hundreds of rotational quanta and the effect washes out entirely. Buffer-gas cooling to 30 K populates the low-J levels where the symmetry physics is unambiguous. And the related result is that the icosahedral fine structure, predicted in 1986, is what let you determine rotational ergodicity breaking — a system failing to explore its own accessible phase space without any symmetry being broken."],
["tech","What would you do with 2.5 watts of mid-infrared that you cannot do with milliwatts?","A test of whether you noticed the 2025 OPO paper. Almost no outside candidate will have. Asking or answering this well marks you instantly.","Power changes which experiments are possible, not just which ones are faster. Three things come to mind. First, much weaker transitions become accessible — overtones, combination bands, and forbidden or weakly allowed transitions you would previously never see above the noise. Second, saturation and double-resonance become realistic: with enough intensity you can saturate a transition and do labelling experiments to prove which lines share a common level, which is exactly how you resolve an ambiguous assignment. Third, far more dilute species — bigger radicals, larger protonated clusters, lower-yield discharge products — come within reach, because you are no longer trading signal against concentration. I would want to ask you which of those three you consider the real payoff, because it would tell me a lot about where the programme is going."],
["sci","How would your microwave work actually help an infrared group?","The central question of your candidacy. If you cannot make this concrete in thirty seconds, the fit argument collapses.","Resolution and which states we see. Your sub-Doppler infrared resolution is around 60 MHz, which is remarkable for direct absorption, and it gives you band origins, vibrationally excited state constants and predissociation linewidths. Rotational spectroscopy reaches a few kHz, but only on the ground state. So the two are complementary rather than competing: I measure ground-state rotational and centrifugal-distortion constants, and nuclear quadrupole coupling, to a precision your technique cannot reach, and you measure the vibrational dynamics mine cannot touch. Put them in a single global fit for the same complex and the resulting potential is better determined than either dataset gives alone. Concretely: you published the IR of Ar2HF to probe three-body forces; I'm doing Ne2HCl and Ne3HCl in the microwave. Those are the same question measured from both ends."],
["sci","What does nuclear quadrupole coupling actually measure?","Your own specialty, so he will expect a clean, physical answer — not a definition recited from a textbook.","It measures the electric field gradient at the nucleus, projected onto the molecular frame. A nucleus with spin greater than one half has a non-spherical charge distribution, so it has a quadrupole moment, and that moment couples to the gradient of the electric field created by the surrounding electrons. The observable is a hyperfine splitting pattern. What makes it useful in a weakly bound complex is that the quadrupole coupling constant is essentially a property of the free monomer, so any change on complexation tells you how the monomer is oriented and how far it is librating — it is a direct probe of the angular potential and of zero-point amplitude. That is why the nitrogen-14 constant in N2-HCl shifts on deuteration: the potential surface hasn't changed at all, but the heavier deuterium has smaller zero-point amplitude, so the molecule samples a different average orientation and the projected field gradient changes. It is one of the cleanest experimental handles on vibrational averaging that exists."],
["sci","Why does deuterating the hydrogen change a constant on the nitrogen?","A sharp, physical question he could easily ask about your own data. The answer must be about zero-point motion, not about electronic structure.","Nothing electronic changes — the potential energy surface is identical, because it depends on nuclear charges, not masses. What changes is the amplitude of the motion on that surface. Deuterium is twice the mass, so its zero-point amplitude in the intermolecular bending and librating coordinates is smaller, and the complex is more tightly confined near its equilibrium geometry. The quadrupole coupling constant we actually measure is an expectation value over the vibrational wavefunction, so a narrower wavefunction means less angular averaging and a projected field gradient closer to the equilibrium value. In my data the averaged nitrogen-14 constant goes from about -4.529 MHz in N2-HCl to a fitted average near -4.581 MHz on deuteration — the magnitude increases, which is what you'd expect from reduced averaging. It is a mass effect on the wavefunction, not a chemical effect on the surface."],
["sci","Your spectrometer covers 2 to 8 GHz. Isn't that quite narrow?","Testing whether you understand your own instrument's limitations — which is a proxy for whether you understand instruments at all.","It is narrow, and it sets what I can study. At 2 to 8 GHz I'm looking at low-J transitions of fairly heavy complexes — small rotational constants, so the lines land in band. That is exactly right for rare-gas and nitrogen complexes of HCl and for hydrated clusters, which is why those are my systems. What it rules out is light molecules, whose lowest transitions sit far above 8 GHz. The flip side is that the resonant-cavity design buys me kHz linewidths and the sensitivity to resolve chlorine and nitrogen hyperfine structure, which a broadband chirped-pulse instrument gives up some of for coverage. If I were specifying a next instrument I'd want chirped-pulse capability for survey work and keep the cavity for precision follow-up on anything I found."],
["you","Why this group? You could do rotational spectroscopy anywhere.","He is screening for whether you want him specifically or want a position generally. Vague answers here are fatal and he hears them constantly.","Two specific reasons. The first is molecular: the systems I work on are the systems you built your career on. Rare-gas-HCl complexes, Rg2 and Rg3-HX trimers, N2-HX, water complexes. I don't have to translate my work into your language. The second is methodological, and it's the one I actually came for: I measure ground states to kHz, and I can run variational nuclear-motion calculations in NITROGEN, but I have no access to vibrational dynamics. Your slit jet is the instrument that provides it. The combination of microwave precision, infrared dynamics and a proper nuclear-motion treatment on a computed surface is a complete description of a floppy complex, and it is a combination that currently takes two groups. I'd rather be in the group where the infrared half already exists."],
["you","Walk me through something that went wrong in your own work and what you did.","His real screening question, in whatever form it takes. A group that publishes uncertainty in the fourth decimal place runs on intellectual honesty.","The nitrogen-14 constant in N2-DCl. I couldn't fit it initially — my resolution wasn't sufficient to separate the two nitrogen environments, so the fit wouldn't converge with both floated. What I did first was constrain the two nitrogens to be equal, simulate at several trial values, and compare against the observed spectrum near 3485 to 3488 MHz by eye. That gave about -4.61 MHz, and I built a three-slide comparison showing the observed spectrum overlaid against simulations at -4.529 and -4.61. Later, with better data, I floated both constants independently and got a fitted average of -4.581 MHz. I superseded my own earlier number. The lesson I took is that a visually-chosen parameter is a placeholder, not a result, and it needs to be labelled as one in your own notes so you don't quietly promote it later. That's also why I'd rather tell you this story than a success story."],
["you","Your degrees are in engineering. Convince me.","It is the most obvious question on your record. Being ready for it turns your biggest weakness into a demonstration of self-awareness.","My degrees are electrical and electronic engineering, then industrial and systems engineering. My research is rotational spectroscopy: eight isotopologues of N2-HCl including the nitrogen-14 quadrupole analysis, Ne2HCl and Ne3HCl trimers with potential surfaces computed in NITROGEN, hydrohalic-acid-water clusters, and I've been implementing the Qian-Howard effective Hamiltonian for open-shell O2 complexes. The route in was unusual. The electronics half of it is directly useful — I build and debug my own hardware rather than waiting for someone else to. The systems half means I'm comfortable running a long multi-stage build to a schedule, which is most of what standing up a new optical setup actually is. I'd rather be judged on the spectra and the fits, and I have those with me."],
["you","What do you want to be doing in five years?","Standard, but he is listening for whether your answer requires his group or merely tolerates it.","Running my own high-resolution spectroscopy programme, and I want it to be a programme that doesn't have to choose between precision and dynamics. Right now I can measure a ground state beautifully and say almost nothing about what happens when the complex is vibrationally excited. I want to come out of a postdoc or a PhD able to build both halves — a microwave cavity and a slit-jet infrared spectrometer with a decent light source — and able to do the nuclear-motion theory myself rather than outsourcing it. Your group is the only place I've found where the infrared instrument, the molecular systems I already know, and a culture of building your own source all coexist."],
["tech","How do you prove a line belongs to the species you think it does?","The core craft question of radical spectroscopy, and the thing that goes wrong most often in practice.","Several independent handles, and you want at least two to agree. Concentration modulation first: modulate the discharge and demodulate the signal, so anything that doesn't depend on the discharge drops out — that separates radical from precursor immediately. Then precursor dependence: change the precursor to one that should give the same radical by a different route, or to one that shouldn't give it at all. Then isotopic substitution, which shifts rotational constants in a predictable way and is probably the most convincing single test. Then internal consistency of the fit: combination differences have to close, and the ground-state constants you derive have to match independent values if any exist. And nuclear spin statistics, where available, are very powerful — in your CH2OH work the exchange of the two methyl hydrogens constrains which symmetry species can appear, which made the assignment unambiguous rather than merely plausible."],
["tech","What is the limiting noise source in a direct-absorption experiment?","He quotes noise-equivalent absorption sensitivity in his papers. This asks whether you think like an experimentalist or a data consumer.","In practice, laser amplitude and frequency noise converted to amplitude noise, not detector shot noise — you are almost never shot-noise limited in a real setup. The fractional absorbances are tiny, often parts in a million, so you are looking for a small change on a large background and anything that makes the background wander hurts you directly. The standard countermeasures are a balanced reference beam to cancel common-mode laser noise, modulation at a frequency above the laser's flicker noise, and phase-sensitive lock-in detection to narrow the effective bandwidth. Etalon fringes from stray reflections are the other classic killer, because they drift slowly and look exactly like real spectral features. The honest figure of merit is noise-equivalent absorption sensitivity per root hertz, which is why you quote it rather than quoting a signal-to-noise ratio on your best line."],
["tech","How would you use machine learning here without fooling yourself?","If you raise your ML background, this follows immediately. He is checking for appropriate suspicion of your own tools.","By keeping it on the search half of the problem and off the inference half. The place it genuinely helps is combinatorial: generating candidate assignments for a congested line list, or proposing which of several Hamiltonian models to try first — tasks where the cost of a wrong suggestion is a few minutes and the final arbiter is still a least-squares fit with proper uncertainties. The place it would be dangerous is anywhere it could absorb a systematic error and make it look like signal. A model trained on fitted spectra will happily learn your etalon fringes. So the rules I'd want: the model proposes, the fit decides; combination differences must close independently of anything the model said; and I'd always want to see the residuals myself rather than a score. I'd rather save three weeks of assignment time than claim a result no one can check."],
["sci","Why do you care about three-body forces?","Your trimer work needs a motivation that is not merely 'nobody has measured it'.","Because pairwise additivity is the assumption underneath almost all of condensed-phase modelling, and the trimer is the smallest system where you can test it cleanly. In a dimer you measure a pair potential. In a trimer the total interaction is not the sum of the three pairs, and the difference — exchange and induction contributions — is the first increment of genuine many-body physics. The experimental point is that it is small, so you need high precision to see it, and the systems have to be weakly bound enough that the non-additive part is a meaningful fraction. That is the argument for neon rather than argon: weaker binding, larger zero-point amplitude, and non-additivity relatively more important. Your Ar2HF and Ar2CO2 work established that this was measurable at all; Ne2HCl and Ne3HCl push it into the regime where it should matter more."],
["strat","What would you want to work on in your first six months?","Testing whether you have a plan or just enthusiasm. Over-specifying is as bad as under-specifying.","I'd want to split it. Most of the time learning the slit-jet instrument properly from whoever runs it — alignment, the discharge, the modulation chain, the frequency calibration — because I can't propose anything credible about an instrument I haven't run. Alongside that, something I can contribute from week one: taking a complex where you already have infrared data and I or someone else has ground-state microwave constants, and attempting a combined global fit. That's a low-risk, self-contained project that uses what I already have while I'm learning what I don't. If it works it's a paper; if it doesn't, I've learned the instrument anyway. What I'd want to avoid is proposing a brand-new experiment before I understand why the existing one is built the way it is."],
["strat","What don't you know that worries you?","Deliberately disarming. The wrong answer is a humblebrag; the right answer is specific and shows you have already started closing the gap.","The optics, concretely. I've read the 2025 OPO paper closely enough to describe the architecture — singly-resonant ring cavity, 50 mm fanout PPLN, signal locked to a Fabry-Perot referenced to a stabilized HeNe, over 2.5 watts across 3 to 4 microns — and I understand why quasi-phase-matching lets you tune by translating the crystal. But I have never aligned a nonlinear cavity and I have no feel for what breaks, how long a realignment takes, or what the failure modes look like at 2 in the morning. That's a hands-on gap and reading won't close it. The second thing is that I've never worked with species that live for microseconds; all my carriers have been stable. I expect the assignment discipline to transfer and the production chemistry not to."],
["curve","You realize most of what my group does has nothing to do with your work?","A pressure test. He wants to see whether you fold, bluster, or answer accurately.","Yes — two of the four programmes are things I've never touched, and I'd be a poor fit for the single-molecule biophysics side in particular. I'm not applying to the whole lab; I'm applying to the high-resolution spectroscopy programme, where I think the overlap is unusually exact rather than merely plausible. What I'd say in defence of the breadth is that it's one of the reasons I want to be here rather than somewhere narrower. The same philosophy runs through all four — prepare a system in a well-defined state, perturb it, measure the rate — and being in a group that applies it to a liquid interface and a single RNA molecule as well as to a radical is how you learn to recognize the pattern rather than just the technique."],
["curve","Suppose you get a spectrum you cannot assign at all. Then what?","How you behave when the experiment is not working, which is most of the time.","First I'd stop trusting my own assumptions about what's in the jet and check the obvious experimental things: is the carrier what I think it is, am I seeing precursor, are there etalon fringes masquerading as lines, is the frequency axis right. Then I'd try to get model-independent information before attempting any assignment — combination differences to extract ground-state spacings without needing a band origin, and intensity patterns and branch structure to pin down the band type. Then isotopic substitution, which is usually the thing that breaks it open, because the predicted shifts are a strong constraint. If it still won't go I'd want a calculation — predicted rotational constants and band origins from decent theory, even approximate ones, narrow the search enormously. And if all of that failed I'd write it up as an unassigned spectrum with the frequencies tabulated, because someone with a better potential surface in five years can use it. An honest line list is worth more than a wrong assignment."],
["curve","What's wrong with one of my papers?","Rare, but he is capable of it, and most candidates panic. Do not invent a flaw, and do not refuse to engage.","I wouldn't claim to have found an error — I'd be surprised if I had. What I'd point at instead is a limitation the papers themselves acknowledge. The cyclobutyl and cyclopentyl papers both end at the same place: you've established the shape of the puckering potential from the spectroscopy, but the treatment is still effectively an effective-Hamiltonian fit, and the double-minimum problem really wants a variational calculation on a computed surface before you can call the barrier height a measurement rather than an inference. That's not a criticism, it's an open task — and it happens to be the task I'd most like to work on, because it's exactly what NITROGEN is built for."],
["curve","Why should I take you over someone with three first-author J. Chem. Phys. papers?","The hardest question. Comparative, and you cannot win on volume.","On paper you probably shouldn't, if the only axis is publication count. The argument for me is that I bring a capability the group currently has to go outside for, rather than more of the capability it already has. There are many strong infrared spectroscopists. There are fewer people who have measured nuclear quadrupole hyperfine structure in the exact complexes you published potential surfaces for, and who can run variational nuclear-motion calculations on a computed surface in NITROGEN. The cyclobutyl paper's open question is a large-amplitude-motion problem; that's the problem I'd be most useful on from the first month. If what you need is another pair of expert hands on the slit jet, I'd be slower than the obvious candidate. If what you need is the ground-state precision and the theory half, I'd be faster."]
];

const QACATS=[["all","All questions"],["sci","Science depth"],["tech","Technique & craft"],["you","About you & fit"],["strat","Plans & motivation"],["curve","Curveballs & pressure"]];

